sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch05-24> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The biconcave mammalian red blood corpuscle is not a surface of constant mean curvature and so cannot be a fluid drop in equilibrium under surface tension alone; some other force, possibly osmotic inequilibrium or fluid-crystal forces of its cholesterin-like constituents, must maintain its shape, which changes to a sphere in diluted blood.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The biconcave red blood corpuscle is not a surface-tension equilibrium figure";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "biconcave corpuscle: 1/r_1 + 1/r_2 \\neq \\text{const}, so not a fluid drop in equilibrium under surface tension alone";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter V, “The Forms of Cells”; topic “Red blood corpuscles”. Scope: mammalian and other vertebrate red blood cells"
}
